The spec asks for camelCase to become kebab-case: a hyphen goes in front of every internal uppercase letter, and then the whole string is lowercased.

The submission uses a substitution with a callback that looks at the match position:

        def repl(m):
            return ('-' + m.group(0)) if m.start() > 0 else m.group(0)
        return re.sub(r'[A-Z]', repl, s).lower()

The pattern matches each uppercase ASCII letter individually. For every match, the callback prepends a hyphen unless the match starts at index 0, which is how the word internal from the spec is honoured: a capital at the very beginning gets no leading hyphen. After all substitutions the string is lowercased, turning the inserted capitals into their lowercase forms. Lowercase letters and other characters pass through untouched.

The asserts only use capitals that are each followed by lowercase letters. I tried backgroundColorValue, which becomes background-color-value, and the minimal aB, which becomes a-b. For a string beginning with a capital, HelloWorld, the result is hello-world with no hyphen at the front, because the first match is at position zero. With consecutive capitals, as in getHTTPResponse, each capital is an internal uppercase letter, so each receives its own hyphen and the output is get-h-t-t-p-response; that is what a literal reading of the rule gives, and the code applies it consistently. A string with no capitals, like x, is returned unchanged.

The callback treats every capital by the same position rule and makes no reference to the example strings.

